{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>As the children of Esau did to me.</b> Rashi's explanation: not regarding passing through their land, but regarding selling food and water — that is, <b>as the children of Esau did to me</b> is not connected to 'let me pass through your land,' since it is written 'and Edom refused to give Israel passage through his border.' Rather, it is connected to 'I will buy food with money, etc.' — meaning, <b>as</b> the children of Esau <b>sold to me</b> food and water. And by reasoning we may say that the children of Esau sold them food and water, since it is written above, 'And HaShem said to me,' etc., 'you shall buy food from them.' And the meaning of <b>as the children of Esau who dwell in Seir did to me, and the Moabites who dwell in Ar</b> — this implies that these came forward [with provisions] just as those did. And what is written in the portion Ki Tetze, 'because they did not come forward' — that refers only to 'an Ammonite shall not come.' And 'and whom he hired' refers to the Moabites. Another interpretation: ordinary Moabites did not come forward, but those who dwelt in Ar did come forward in the matter of commerce, because Israel passed by them, as it is written, 'which turned to dwell in Ar.' And this is what is written: <b>as the children of Esau who dwell in Seir did to me, and the Moabites who dwell in Ar.</b> But according to this interpretation, there is a difficulty, for in Yevamot, in the chapter HaArel, it is explicitly implied that they sought to disqualify David, who descended from Ruth the Moabitess, on account of the matter that 'they did not come forward.' Therefore one may say: 'I will buy food with money, etc.' through 'let me pass through on foot' — these are the words of the embassy [message] and no more, and the passages in the portion Chukat prove this. But from here onward, they are the words of Moses, who was reminding Israel of things that had already been, and one must rearrange the verses: 'and Sihon king of Heshbon did not consent to let us pass through it' — <b>as the children of Esau did to me, and the Moabites</b> — who did not let us pass through. This is like the example that Rashi explained in the portion Vayerei, regarding 'and HaShem said, if now I have found favor in your eyes.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The abbreviation חז״ק likely stands for חזק קשה ('this is a difficulty'); rendered here as 'there is a difficulty.' The phrase צריך לסרס המקראות means one must cut/rearrange the scriptural verses out of their sequential order, here rendered 'rearrange the verses.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:51:35.809014211Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}